        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-09T18:55:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "When you say \"order\", what do you mean by that? For the two unsized arrays doesn\u0027t this correspond to the binding locations used by the API-side or am I misunderstanding? How is that different from a texture\u0027s binding location?",
      "parentUuid": "6a5277e8_e18317f3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 1,
        "endChar": 15
      },
      "revId": "39434936f3ef13ec8665cb3eecee754710b70420",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3fb018fe_8efee8c7",
        "filename": "resources/sksl/compute/ArrayAdd.compute",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 1,
      "author": {
        "id": 5640
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-10T13:36:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I mean it in the most literal sense possible - it\u0027s the order of the compute function parameters.\n\nIf you specify:\n\nlayout(location\u003d0) in int x;\nlayout(location\u003d1) out int y;\n\nyou get a compute function with the signature computeMain(int x, int* y). It\u0027s important to recognize that there aren\u0027t even necessarily buffers involved at all, since compute functions can accept scalars just fine.",
      "parentUuid": "529e1152_c0ff2250",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 1,
        "endChar": 15
      },
      "revId": "39434936f3ef13ec8665cb3eecee754710b70420",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4c214804_c1d97062",
        "filename": "resources/sksl/compute/ArrayAdd.compute",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 2,
      "author": {
        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-08T22:19:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t necessarily want to hold back this CL for this, as it\u0027s a great first pass, but I\u0027m still not a fan of `in` and `out` in this context. When used with global variables and blocks, `in`/`out` in GLSL (and the corresponding `Input`/`Output` storage class decorators in SPIR-V) are used with entry point IO. Someone with a GL background may read this as denoting a different instance of `src1` for each invocation of `main()` but there is really one global `src1` that is shared across invocations. A compute stage doesn\u0027t necessarily have prescribed pipeline input/output and IIUC we are using these keywords really as a way to specify \"read/write access mode\".\n\nEventually we will want to enable uniforms in compute shaders. AFAIK textures/samplers used in compute shaders are already decorated as being in UniformConstant storage class in Vulkan and would be declared as `uniform` in GLSL. Similarly, GLSL storage blocks use the `buffer` keyword and look more or less like uniforms in code.\n\nWhy not start out with allowing `uniform` and `buffer` keywords to specify the address spaces? We could then use interface blocks and global declarations as we do for fragment/vertex shaders and the code would feel familiar.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 2,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "39434936f3ef13ec8665cb3eecee754710b70420",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1c6bbb08_22afaabf",
        "filename": "resources/sksl/compute/ArrayAdd.compute",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 2,
      "author": {
        "id": 5640
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-09T15:02:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There\u0027s a followup CL coming which will add explicit address space modifiers, following the Metal model (device, thread, threadgroup, constant). Likewise with uniforms, Metal doesn\u0027t even have uniforms, so I\u0027m not necessarily convinced there\u0027s a lot of value in adding support for them in this context.",
      "parentUuid": "4c214804_c1d97062",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 2,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "39434936f3ef13ec8665cb3eecee754710b70420",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cae84848_880a67ea",
        "filename": "resources/sksl/compute/ArrayAdd.compute",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 2,
      "author": {
        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-09T18:55:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Metal is special, in that there everything is a \"buffer\" but OpenGL, Vulkan, and WebGPU all have uniforms in their compute shaders. In GLSL (OpenGL and Vulkan) the `in` keyword is used only for varyings like gl_GlobalInvocationID and _resources_ are declared as one would declare any good-old SSBO or uniform.\n\nI was under the impression that we wanted to stay close to GLSL. It\u0027s OK if we want to abandon that stance but I think I\u0027d like to see a plan for how all of the language idioms we are introducing now will eventually map to platforms other than Metal, especially if we\u0027re exposing Metal-style address spaces in SkSL.",
      "parentUuid": "1c6bbb08_22afaabf",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 2,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "39434936f3ef13ec8665cb3eecee754710b70420",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "87d587c8_73e0bdec",
        "filename": "resources/sksl/compute/ArrayAdd.compute",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 2,
      "author": {
        "id": 5640
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-10T13:36:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not *opposed* to adding uniforms, I\u0027m just saying that I\u0027m not necessarily convinced it\u0027s an important feature in this context. If it turns out to be, great, we add them. It\u0027s not particularly hard, we just follow the existing model of bundling them together into a struct and passing them in as a hidden parameter.\n\nAnd my understanding was that the Metal address spaces map pretty directly to the address spaces used by other APIs; I was only really referring to the naming convention when I said \"the Metal model\". Am I mistaken?",
      "parentUuid": "cae84848_880a67ea",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 2,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "39434936f3ef13ec8665cb3eecee754710b70420",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "16fe823f_37137dca",
        "filename": "resources/sksl/compute/ArrayAdd.compute",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 2,
      "author": {
        "id": 5631
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-10T14:19:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think there are (at least) two orthogonal concepts. One is the address space stuff, which appears to have slightly different names, but similar semantics across all APIs. (I haven\u0027t looked exhaustively, but that\u0027s my first read of things).\n\nThe other is how we expose any kind of resources that have corresponding API for binding on the calling code. I also anticipate that we\u0027re going to need uniform buffers eventually, but from a compute perspective, that\u0027s just a special case of buffer. Ultimately, each API has made some decisions over the years about how to declare resources (buffers, textures, samplers, etc...) - both from a syntactic PoV (are they args to main or globals), and what names and namespaces they use to enumerate the elements of each type.\n\nWe started out with GLSL, so we inherited some of their quirks, but now we have a bit of a hodge-podge. Knowing that we\u0027re targeting a variety of APIs, it would be great to abstract this stuff -- our decision about people indicate the number, type, and layout of resources doesn\u0027t need to be tied to any API -- and having a unified higher-level model should make it easier to map to each specific backend.\n\nTo that point: I think it\u0027s really worth looking at WGPU/WGSL here, because they had to solve this *exact* same problem -- their model of resource binding describes everything using two annotations per variable: \u0027group\u0027 and \u0027binding\u0027 (https://www.w3.org/TR/WGSL/#resource-interface). They put all bindable resources in the same space, and can obviously splat those out to use whatever model makes the most sense in SPIR-V, MSL, HLSL, etc.\n\n(Note that they also have a \u0027location\u0027 annotation, but that\u0027s *only* for varyings between the vertex and fragment stages, and isn\u0027t even allowed in compute).",
      "parentUuid": "87d587c8_73e0bdec",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 2,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "39434936f3ef13ec8665cb3eecee754710b70420",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
